title: batch commands abort on the first bad id, violating karr's own ADR 0002
updated: 2026-08-10T02:39:53Z
---

`move`, `edit` and `delete` `die` on a missing id **inside** the loop
(`Cmd/Move.pm:86`, `Cmd/Edit.pm:153`, `Cmd/Delete.pm:65`), so every remaining id
is skipped. `archive` alone does it right (`Cmd/Archive.pm:51-52`: warn and
collect).

    karr list --compact     # #1 t1  #2 t2  #3 t3
    karr move 999,3 todo    -> rc=1 "Task 999 not found";  #3 never processed
    karr delete 999,3 --yes -> rc=1, nothing deleted
    karr edit 999,3 -a note -> rc=1, nothing edited
    karr archive 999,3      -> rc=1, but task 3 IS archived

The outcome depends on the order of the ids: `move 1,999 todo` moves 1,
`move 999,1 todo` moves nothing.

ADR 0002 states it explicitly: *"Batch semantics stay: partial success is
committed, the exit code reports the failure (1)."* So this is a violation of
karr's own documented contract, not just a parity gap. kanban-md's `runBatch`
(`cmd/root.go:199-238`) iterates every id, collects per-id results and returns 1
at the end.

There is a second, sharper edge on `delete`: because the `die` happens inside the
loop, `sync_after` never runs, so refs already deleted locally never reach the
remote (the #37 END flush pushes them, but the command has already reported
failure).

Fix: one shared batch runner used by all four commands — iterate, collect,
report `Completed N/M`, exit 1 if any id failed.
Re-verified against main at 3dac607, and it is worse than filed on one point:

    $ karr move 4,999,5 todo
    Moved task 4: backlog -> todo
    Task 999 not found
    $ echo $?
    0
    $ karr list --compact
    #4          todo a
    #5       backlog b

Task 5 is never reached -- so it is not only "aborts on the first bad id", the
command also exits 0 while having half-applied the batch and reported a failure
on stderr. A caller checking the exit code is told everything worked.

Per karr ADR 0002 the batch should apply every valid id and report the invalid
ones, and the exit code has to reflect that something failed.
CORRECTION to my note above: the "exits 0" claim is WRONG and was my own
measurement error. I ran `karr move 4,999,5 todo 2>&1 | head -4; echo rc=$?`,
where $? reports the exit status of `head`, not of karr. Re-measured without the
pipe:

    $ out=$(karr move 1,999,2 todo 2>&1); echo $?
    1

The agent working this ticket independently failed to reproduce exit 0 across
four configurations (with and without a remote, current and installed karr) and
was right to push back rather than take my word for it.

What IS real, and is what this ticket is about: move, edit and delete die inside
the loop, so every id after the bad one is skipped, and the behaviour is
order-dependent -- `move 1,999` moves 1, `move 999,1` moves nothing. archive
already warned and continued. The exit code was never the defect.
Fixed and merged into main (228fffa); full suite green at 98 files / 670 tests.

#61: the exit-code half of my earlier note was my own measurement error (see the
correction above) -- the real defect was the loop dying on the first bad id and
the resulting order-dependence. Contract now: per-id runtime failures warn,
continue, and exit 1 with an "N of M ids failed" summary; a usage error aborts
the batch at exit 2 with nothing written, because it is wrong for every id at
once and collecting it would demote a 2 to a 1.

#97: archive is on the guarded path; handoff had already adopted check_claim in
an earlier merge, but its read-then-write was still unguarded and is now a
compare-and-swap. Verified afterwards that store->save_task_cas has exactly one
caller, Role::BoardAccess::save_task -- no command reaches around the door.
